We introduce novel approaches for visually identifying and validating robust redshifts derived from emission lines in submillimeter observations. Our key findings include: 
(1) Emission line flux measurements are highly susceptible to noise; 
(2) Flux estimates are often uncertain and easily distorted by extended noise patterns; 
(3) Scattering of Lyman-$\alpha$ emission is a critical indicator of rotation but is also prone to noise; and 
(4) Flux information from the Lyman-$\alpha$ and C\,IV lines is unreliable due to contamination from the ongoing intergalactic medium.

